# BUGS

A 9P call for a file's metadata (a walk, a stat, an open, a clunk) keeps the back end's lock until it is answered; a server that holds one holds every thread.
Seven calls held on one connection at once, such as seven threads reading terminals of one ptyd, make an eighth call on it wait for one of them.

There is no `/bin/sh`, and `exec` runs only ELF programs, so `system`, `popen`, `make`'s recipes, Lua's `os.execute` and `io.popen`, `env`, cron, and `ed`'s and `dc`'s `!` fail.

Everyone is root: `getuid` and its kin answer 0, and a file's owner and group read as 0, though fsd keeps owners and checks permissions (M6 step 6e).
There is no `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/group`, `/etc/services`, `/dev/log` or `/dev/tty`, and no `LOGNAME`, and `ttyname` needs `/proc/self/fd`, so `chown`, `chgrp`, `whoami`, `logname`, `logger`, `tty` and others fail or misbehave.
`faccessat` opens the file in the mode asked, as 9front's APE, and makes and removes a probe file `_AcChAcK`<pid> to answer a directory's `W_OK`; there are no effective ids, so `AT_EACCESS` changes nothing.
`st_dev` is the connection in the process's namespace (a slot an unmount frees may come again for another server).
`flock`'s locks: see lock(5) and flock(1).

Not implemented: `link` and `linkat` (`EPERM`), `mknod`, `chroot`, `sethostname`, and `clock_settime` (`ENOSYS`).
`getrlimit` answers infinity for every limit.
CPU time is the kernel's 10 ms samples (ADR-0041): `times`, `getrusage`, `wait4`'s rusage and the process's and thread's CPU-time clocks report it; another thread's or process's clock is refused, and children's times are lost across `execve`. Priorities: `getpriority` answers 0 and `setpriority` to any other value is `EPERM`.

Signals have no `sigqueue` or real-time queueing, and `sigaltstack` no `SS_AUTODISARM`.
A handler without `SA_ONSTACK` may run on the alternate stack, where the kernel put the note, if the thread has one.
`execve` in a process with more than one thread fails: the kernel's exec wants the caller alone, and nothing ends the others first.
Job control is partial: no `SIGTTIN` or `SIGTTOU`, no controlling terminal kept for a session, and anyone who can write a terminal's `ctl` can set its foreground group.

Sockets are IPv4 only, with no `AF_UNIX` and no `socketpair`; `getaddrinfo` reads `/etc/resolv.conf` and `/etc/hosts`, which the namespace lacks, so only numeric addresses work; a bound port is used only by `listen`; a UDP datagram is at most 16 KiB.
There is no `epoll` or `kqueue`.

`mprotect` is `as_protect`, and `PROT_NONE` maps no page, so a guard page faults; `MAP_SHARED` anonymous memory is shared with forked children (ADR-0042). `mremap` grows a private mapping in place where the pages after it are free, else moves and copies it (to `MREMAP_FIXED`'s address too); a shared mapping is neither grown nor moved (`EINVAL`), as a copy would not be shared. A mapping past a file's last page faults with `SIGSEGV`, not `SIGBUS`; `msync(MS_SYNC)` does not wait; at most 64 files are mapped at once.
`fork` copies all of a process's memory at once.

An offset is shared with a child only on a server with `posix`; elsewhere the child opens the file again.
Closing a descriptor that a `dup2` or `dup3` replaces does not drop the file's locks.
A pipe is a channel of 4 KiB messages, and a writer to a full pipe polls.
`O_APPEND` is atomic only on a server with `posix`.
musl's `mktemp` names are guessable.
